#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444067} :activity/id #uuid "cf6c0bba-9840-5282-9c1e-6b17cae9759d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048817 :topic/id #uuid "c277f404-7746-5317-a99f-359354eb8c6a" :topic/math-academy-id 6682} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444065 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3937af2-b650-5eaa-a987-e97fd137f52f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\cos(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444058}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444058} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8e8dcc5-5048-58a4-a50f-532f28ef34ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6c38c2df-a1c4-591d-95c2-93cd739693be" :question/math-academy-id "q-334973" :question/problem "Given that $P(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${P}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${P}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${P}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nP(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))^{2}] & =2(x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =2(x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)(x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)(x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t){\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-1}^{1}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot x{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =0+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0b5c3e8c-b479-5b7c-995d-1b489fe05efc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444069}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444066 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444063 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49bfc920-4798-5ac8-829c-10c6425543fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sin(t)-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444056}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444056} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4f3822a-92a6-5439-9a5d-bf0f8c9a91bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5b2ce271-5eac-5d80-b8db-22d863d22bcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-352887" :question/problem "Given that $K(t)={\\int }_{0}^{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-3x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute ${K}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nK(t)={\\int }_{0}^{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-3x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-3x)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-3x)^{2}] & =2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-3x)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-3x) \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-3x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t){\\int }_{0}^{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{4}3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\,x{|}_{0}^{4}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot (4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-24) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "187e39d4-f8d9-5968-b9f2-0918091fc0cc"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444067 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba4b594e-6e46-5106-afac-33e43b94aed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36t+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444057}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444057} :answer-field/id #uuid "831d751e-c629-5b32-9f44-368844ac1a78" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2a7ae8c6-87fd-5aaa-b1ca-f181d86f7e93" :question/math-academy-id "q-334936" :question/problem "Given that $P(t)={\\int }_{-2}^{0}(x-3t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${P}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${P}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${P}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nP(t)={\\int }_{-2}^{0}(x-3t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x-3t)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x-3t)^{2}] & =2(x-3t)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(x-3t) \\\\  & =2(x-3t)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-6(x-3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}-6(x-3t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(x-3t)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(x-3t)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-2}^{0}3t\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-2}^{0}-3tx{|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =-2-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(t) & =-6(-2-6t) \\\\  & =12(3t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "452238fc-0a14-5395-9d29-80eea1eaebf3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444065}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444068 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f1d1d5-26f2-5996-9b45-e843073eebd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308784} {:answer/id #uuid "105ce355-6bc0-5eac-ab1d-30bf73c3e3ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308865} {:answer/id #uuid "1489c33b-6f54-52d8-aae5-e12710732f04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308900} {:answer/id #uuid "8dad0b51-3e0e-5303-9848-e926f7285ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309871} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8570d1-eab0-562a-97e3-ada215b2c56d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310757}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308784} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4cc132b-f46c-51a6-9635-8bd3d3a39e29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b7963456-3a78-5141-94e0-577dece1c8b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-335249" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\nI. ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n\nII. ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{|x-t|}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n\nIII. ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R$ because the denominator is always positive. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=\\frac{2t({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2{t}^{3}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}=\\frac{2t{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ So, differentiating under the integral sign is valid for all $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{|x-t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,1)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that this function is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{2}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{t}^{2}).\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nSince $x\\in [0,1]$ and $t>0,$ we have $x+{t}^{2}>0,$ so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{2t}{x+{t}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid for all $t>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "86e12b8a-080a-53fd-a5ee-62fcb2d5ac6b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444066}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444069 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311550 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1674bcea-39fc-5aad-aca7-372a177fe050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308917} {:answer/id #uuid "25e64bcc-0147-5181-9ef9-b6cb514ee5a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309029} {:answer/id #uuid "511cfe36-d25a-5e4f-af0d-d2735ed975a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309361} {:answer/id #uuid "8d5d3830-b200-5801-9be5-e52c9b90e922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309864} {:answer/id #uuid "955b3dd8-2188-5b71-8408-64ec11de032b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309945}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309864} :answer-field/id #uuid "c85287b0-57f7-5ef2-b815-ce3761ebab78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9de6d57f-11dc-5bb2-bebc-d38ef2773685" :question/math-academy-id "q-335247" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\nI. ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{x+t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n\nII. ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{xt}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n\nIII. ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>1$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\sqrt{x+t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+t}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R$ (since $x+t\\ge c>0$ on $R$). Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{xt}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{x({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2x{t}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>0.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>1$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(1,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that for $t>1$ and $x\\in [0,1],$ the denominator $x-t$ never vanishes on $R,$ so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "c87a8b02-417c-5ed5-80b6-82dec3b064cd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444068}}] :activity/title "Differentiating Under the Integral Sign" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444055}]}
